title **12 · Salesforce sync cycle** — JWT bearer → watermarked extract → Parquet + DuckDB upsert → LanceDB re-index → **watermark committed LAST**\n(evidence: sync-worker/syncworker/main.py:106-194 and :259-301 · sf_auth.py:24-129 · sf_client.py:123-258 · storage.py:92-167 · rag_index.py:97-154)\n 

autonumber "<b>[00]"

box "TRUST BOUNDARY · JWT signing key" #FEF2F2
participant "<$lock>\nsf_jwt_key.pem\n/data/sf_jwt_key.pem" as key #DC2626
end box
participant "<$cogs>\nsync-worker\nsyncworker/main.py" as sw #7C3AED
participant "<$cloud>\nSalesforce org" as sf #64748B
participant "<$hdd>\nParquet\nPARQUET_DIR" as pq #D97706
participant "<$database>\nDuckDB warehouse\n/data/warehouse.duckdb" as duck #D97706
participant "<$microchip>\nvllm-embed\nQwen3-Embedding-0.6B" as emb #16A34A
participant "<$database>\nLanceDB\ntable: chunks" as lance #D97706

loop every SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES (default 30) — main.py:280-291
sw -> duck : ``Store(DUCKDB_PATH)`` — a FRESH connection per cycle (main.py:285)\n``CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS _sync_meta`` (storage.py:80-85)
note left of sw #FEE2E2
  **No restart policy, no HEALTHCHECK.**
  ``load_settings()``/``load_object_configs()`` run **outside** the try
  (main.py:261-262 vs :281): a bad ``SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES`` or a malformed
  ``config.yaml`` kills the process at startup — and ``sync-worker`` is the
  **only** compose service without ``restart:`` (docker-compose.yml:291-334).
end note

== 1 · authenticate — JWT bearer assertion ==

group TokenManager.get_token() — cached 25 min (sf_auth.py:47,58-68)
  alt SF_CLIENT_SECRET is set  (secrets.py:130-138) — client_credentials, **no JWT is built**
    sw -> sf : **POST {SF_LOGIN_URL}/services/oauth2/token** · HTTPS · form · sync\ngrant_type=client_credentials & client_id & client_secret — sf_auth.py:83
  else JWT bearer grant  (the documented path)
    sw -> key : read PEM — ``SF_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE`` | ``SF_PRIVATE_KEY_B64`` |\n``/data/sf_jwt_key.pem`` (secrets.py:140-151) · **file I/O · blocking**
    key --> sw : RSA private key bytes  (``_check_pem`` only greps for "PRIVATE KEY", secrets.py:60-79)
    sw -> sw : ``build_jwt_assertion`` — RS256 over\n{iss: client_id, sub: username, aud: login_url, exp: now+**180**s}\nsf_auth.py:24-39
    note right of sw #FEE2E2
      **Clock skew is entirely unhandled.** ``exp`` is wall-clock
      ``time.time() + 180`` (sf_auth.py:32,37).
      **There is no iat claim, no nbf claim, no jti claim**
      and no skew allowance whatsoever. On a drifting
      air-gapped DGX this fails as an opaque
      ``RuntimeError: … HTTP 400`` (sf_auth.py:121-123) that names
      neither the clock nor Salesforce's ``error_description`` —
      the body parse is wrapped in ``except Exception: error = ""``
      (sf_auth.py:106-109).
    end note
    sw -> sf : **POST {SF_LOGIN_URL}/services/oauth2/token** · HTTPS · form · sync\ngrant_type=jwt-bearer & assertion=<signed JWT> — sf_auth.py:98
  end
  sf --> sw : 200 {access_token, instance_url}\ncached for **TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS = 25 min** — a guess (sf_auth.py:45-47)
  note over sw, sf #FEE2E2
    **No retry, no backoff, no rate-limit awareness on the token POST.**
    One transient 503 from the login host raises ``RuntimeError``, kills
    the whole cycle, and the outer handler backs off 30 s → 30 min
    (main.py:292-299). ``httpx.Client(timeout=30.0)`` (sf_auth.py:53) is
    the only bound — and that client is **never closed**.
  end note
end

== 2 · per configured object (48 in config.yaml) — main.py:106-194 ==

loop for obj in objects  (run_cycle, main.py:239-250 — one failure does not stop the cycle)

  sw -> duck : ``SELECT watermark FROM _sync_meta WHERE object_name = ?``\nstorage.py:92-97 · parameterised
  duck --> sw : watermark  |  NULL
  sw -> sw : ``cycle_start = now(UTC)`` — captured **BEFORE** any network call\nmain.py:115
  note right of sw #ECFDF5
    **Deliberate and correct.** The watermark that will be committed is
    the time the extract *started*, not the time it finished, so records
    modified **during** the extract are re-fetched next cycle. The
    DELETE-by-Id upsert makes that replay idempotent (main.py:186-187).
  end note

  sw -> sf : **GET /services/data/{v}/sobjects/{obj}/describe** · Bearer · sync\nsf_client.py:165
  sf --> sw : field metadata
  sw -> sw : drop fields this user cannot see (main.py:128-136) then\n``adopt_new_fields`` up to SYNC_MAX_FIELDS=80 (main.py:142-145)
  note left of sw #FFFBEB
    ``except Exception`` on describe → keep configured fields
    (main.py:122-126) and ``except Exception: return fields, rag_fields``
    inside adoption (main.py:81-82) — the second logs **nothing at all**.
  end note

  alt watermark IS NULL — first sync → **Bulk API 2.0 full extract**
    sw -> sf : **POST /services/data/{v}/jobs/query** · JSON\n{operation:"query", query: build_full_soql(obj, fields)} — sf_client.py:199
    sf --> sw : {id: jobId}
    loop poll until state == JobComplete  (sf_client.py:208-220)
      sw -> sf : **GET /jobs/query/{id}** · every ``poll_interval`` = 5.0 s
      sf --> sw : {state}  — Failed/Aborted → RuntimeError
    end
    note right of sf #FEE2E2
      **UNBOUNDED POLL LOOP** — ``while True`` with no iteration cap,
      no wall-clock deadline and no shutdown check (sf_client.py:208-220).
      A job stuck in ``InProgress`` spins forever at 5 s, blocking all
      47 other objects: ``run_cycle`` is a plain sequential loop
      (main.py:239-243) and ``flag.stop`` is never read inside a cycle.
    end note
    sf --> sw : **GET /jobs/query/{id}/results** · Accept: text/csv\nmaxRecords=10000, ``Sforce-Locator`` paging — sf_client.py:224-238
  else watermark present — **incremental REST SOQL**
    sw -> sf : **GET /services/data/{v}/query?q=** · Bearer · sync\n``SELECT <fields> FROM <obj> WHERE SystemModstamp > <watermark>``\nsf_client.py:91-101, :244-246
    sf --> sw : {records, done, nextRecordsUrl} — paged at sf_client.py:255-258
  end
  note over sw, sf #FFFBEB
    **Injection is guarded**: object and every field are matched against
    ``_IDENT_RE`` and the watermark against a strict datetime regex
    (sf_client.py:78-83, :96-97). **Read-only is enforced three ways**:
    GET/POST-only (sf_client.py:132-133), bulk operation allow-list
    ``{query, queryAll}`` (:195-196), and no write endpoint exists.
    **The only retry anywhere is a single 401 replay** (sf_client.py:138-143),
    which re-POSTs and can create a **second** Bulk job for the same SOQL.
    5xx, ConnectError, ReadTimeout and 403 REQUEST_LIMIT_EXCEEDED all
    propagate immediately; ``check_api_limits`` (:50-75) only warns at 80 %.
  end note

  loop for each batch yielded lazily by the generator  (main.py:163-184)

    sw -> sw : ``normalize_records(batch)`` — **every value cast to str | None**\nstorage.py:38-56 → every DuckDB column becomes VARCHAR
    sw -> pq : ``write_parquet_batch`` → PARQUET_DIR/<obj>/<obj>_<UTCstamp>_<uuid8>.parquet\nstorage.py:59-68 · **file write · blocking**
    note right of pq #FEE2E2
      **Parquet is NOT idempotent.** Every replay writes *new*
      uuid-named files (storage.py:64-68), so a crashed run's partial
      files stay on disk forever beside the replay's. No retention,
      no compaction, no directory bound anywhere.
    end note

    group ONE DuckDB transaction — storage.py:149-161 · ROLLBACK + re-raise on error (:162-164)
      sw -> duck : ``BEGIN TRANSACTION``
      sw -> duck : ``ALTER TABLE "<obj>" ADD COLUMN …`` for each drifted column (:151-154)
      sw -> duck : ``DELETE FROM "<obj>" WHERE Id IN (SELECT Id FROM _staging_df)`` (:155-157)
      sw -> duck : ``INSERT INTO "<obj>" BY NAME SELECT * FROM _staging_df`` (:158-160)
      sw -> duck : ``COMMIT``
    end
    note right of duck #ECFDF5
      **DuckDB IS idempotent.** DELETE-by-Id then INSERT inside one
      transaction, plus an intra-batch ``drop_duplicates`` on Id keeping
      the last (storage.py:133). Replaying the same batch yields
      the same rows — proven by tests/test_upsert.py:27-61.
      Cost: no PRIMARY KEY or index exists on any per-object table
      (storage.py:140), so the DELETE is a full scan per batch.
    end note

    opt obj has rag_fields  (34 of 48 objects, 61 fields)
      sw -> sw : ``chunk_text(str(value))`` — 800-word windows, 100 overlap\nchunking.py:14-40 · **whitespace-split "tokens"**
      loop for each slice of EMBED_BATCH_SIZE = 32  (rag_index.py:45-46)
        sw -> emb : **POST {EMBED_VIA}/embeddings** · JSON · sync\n{model: EMBED_MODEL, input: [≤32 chunks]} — rag_index.py:47-50
        emb --> sw : {data:[{embedding}, …]}
      end
      note right of emb #FEE2E2
        **GPU embedding blocks Salesforce pagination.** The extract
        generators are lazy (sf_client.py:193-258), so the Bulk result
        locator and the REST ``nextRecordsUrl`` stay open while thousands
        of embeddings compute (rag_index.py concurrency).
        **No retry, no backoff** on this POST (rag_index.py:47-51) — one
        503 from a reloading vLLM aborts the batch's indexing.
        ``timeout=300.0`` **per 32-text batch** (rag_index.py:41), with no
        deadline across the unbounded number of batches.
        **No re-embedding guard**: any ``SystemModstamp`` bump re-embeds
        every chunk of that record — no hash, no modstamp comparison.
      end note
      loop for each changed record id  (rag_index.py:141-142)
        sw -> lance : ``table.delete("record_id = '<Id>'")`` — **one call PER record**\nid pre-validated ``^[a-zA-Z0-9]{15,18}$`` (rag_index.py:26,108)
      end
      sw -> lance : ``table.add(rows)`` — rag_index.py:144
    end
    note over sw, lance #FEE2E2
      **LanceDB is NOT idempotent in the failure direction.** Everything
      this ``opt`` block raises is swallowed at **main.py:174-184**
      (``event=rag_index_error``) — and the watermark **still advances**
      two steps below. Those chunks are never re-indexed until the record
      changes again. Same for a dimension change: ``_open_or_create_table``
      ignores ``dim`` when the table exists (rag_index.py:73-89), so
      ``table.add`` fails on schema mismatch **forever**.
    end note
  end

  == 3 · commit the watermark LAST ==

  sw -> duck : ``set_watermark(obj, cycle_start)`` — **main.py:188** →\n``INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`` (storage.py:99-106) · autocommitted
  note over sw, duck #ECFDF5
    **THE ORDERING IS THE CRASH-SAFETY PROPERTY.**
    ``store.upsert`` commits at **main.py:167**; ``store.set_watermark``
    runs at **main.py:188**, only after the whole ``for batch`` loop
    (main.py:163-184) completes without raising.
    Kill the process anywhere in between → the watermark is unchanged →
    the next cycle re-runs the *same* SOQL and re-upserts the *same* rows,
    and the DELETE-by-Id + INSERT transaction converges. **Correct.**
    Costs of the replay: when the watermark was NULL,
    **the entire Bulk full extract restarts from scratch** — no
    resumability, no partial-progress marker, no reuse of the completed
    job id. Separately, **Salesforce DELETEs are never propagated**
    to DuckDB or LanceDB by any code path in this repo.
  end note
end

sw -> duck : ``store.close()`` — main.py:289 (in a ``finally``)
sw -> sw : ``flag.sleep(interval * 60)`` — main.py:291\non a cycle exception: backoff 30 s → ×2 → cap 30 min (main.py:292-299)
note left of sw #FFFBEB
  Interval drift: the sleep starts *after* the cycle, so the real period is
  ``interval + cycle_duration`` (main.py:290-291). ``docker stop`` mid-extract
  is not honoured — ``flag.stop`` is read only by ``_StopFlag.sleep`` and the
  outer ``while`` (main.py:48, :280), so the container is SIGKILLed.
end note
end

